WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.200
Have you ever been in a conversation where you felt like you were genuinely on the same wavelength, anticipating each other's thoughts, maybe even finishing each other's sentences almost without effort?

00:10.360 --> 00:10.960
Oh, definitely.

00:11.100 --> 00:12.720
That feeling of real synchronicity.

00:12.740 --> 00:12.960
Yeah.

00:13.340 --> 00:14.760
Or, you know, the flip side.

00:14.760 --> 00:22.740
Have you ever hit that frustrating wall trying to explain something, maybe to a friend, feeling totally out of sync, like you were speaking completely different languages?

00:22.860 --> 00:23.120
Absolutely.

00:23.360 --> 00:25.840
Where the connection just isn't happening.

00:25.960 --> 00:27.860
It's like hitting a brick wall conceptually.

00:27.980 --> 00:28.340
Exactly.

00:28.340 --> 00:40.820
What if that profound sense of understanding or, well, that frustrating disconnect actually involves something deeper, maybe a more embodied form of connection than we usually think about?

00:41.060 --> 00:42.900
It's a really compelling thought, isn't it?

00:43.240 --> 00:47.140
We tend to think about connection in purely mental or emotional terms.

00:47.320 --> 00:50.900
But what if the foundation is actually, well, physical?

00:51.320 --> 00:51.560
Yeah.

00:51.720 --> 00:52.600
Or maybe energetic?

00:52.600 --> 00:53.060
Right.

00:53.220 --> 00:58.120
What if understanding isn't just, you know, a meeting of minds, but maybe a meeting of bodies?

00:58.760 --> 01:04.120
So today we're embarking on a deep dive into something we're kind of playfully calling mind melding.

01:04.500 --> 01:04.620
Yeah.

01:04.800 --> 01:06.360
Not the Star Trek kind, though.

01:06.520 --> 01:07.120
No, no.

01:07.180 --> 01:08.460
Definitely not psychic powers.

01:08.620 --> 01:22.260
But as a surprisingly tangible concept that seems to pop up across really different fields, we're talking music theory, cutting edge cognitive science, even the complex history of philosophy.

01:22.260 --> 01:25.300
It really does bridge some unexpected areas.

01:25.300 --> 01:37.980
Our mission, then, is to explore how this shared understanding seems to be profoundly rooted in our physical being, our bodies, and what exactly happens when that crucial connection breaks down.

01:37.980 --> 01:44.200
And maybe how we might actually be melding, you know, connecting with the world and each other in ways we don't even consciously realize.

01:44.340 --> 01:47.720
We'll be navigating through some, well, some pretty intricate ideas today.

01:47.920 --> 01:49.400
Yeah, definitely some complex stuff.

01:49.400 --> 02:01.980
But our aim is to really distill those key insights, those moments that make you go, uh-huh, that tie these seemingly separate threads together, basically giving you a shortcut to being really well informed on this fascinating topic.

02:02.180 --> 02:03.400
And it's quite a journey we've mapped out.

02:03.500 --> 02:04.120
It really is.

02:04.520 --> 02:11.900
We'll start with the ideas of music theorist Arnie Cox, this idea that music is a kind of embodied cognition, which is fascinating in itself.

02:11.900 --> 02:25.180
Then we'll pivot to something really interesting, a kind of intellectual drama, the surprising philosophical tensions, these unspoken disagreements that apparently fractured the relationship between two giants, Michel Foucault and Gilles Deleuze.

02:25.320 --> 02:28.560
Yeah, people usually lump them together, but the reality seems much more complex.

02:28.800 --> 02:29.140
Exactly.

02:29.140 --> 02:40.160
And finally, we'll try to connect these human-level things, connection and fracture, to a really groundbreaking new framework for understanding neural representations.

02:40.420 --> 02:41.560
It's called RSVP.

02:41.900 --> 02:44.980
Right, the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum Framework.

02:45.180 --> 02:51.600
It offers this geometric model for how our thoughts can become coherent or, well, frustratingly tangled.

02:52.100 --> 02:55.380
So, yeah, get ready to maybe reconsider how you think about thinking itself.

02:55.960 --> 02:58.820
Okay, let's kick things off with something pretty universal, right?

02:58.860 --> 03:00.640
Something almost primal for a lot of us.

03:01.140 --> 03:01.580
Music.

03:02.020 --> 03:08.160
Whether it's, I don't know, a huge symphony, a driving rock beat, a sad folk song, music just moves us.

03:08.240 --> 03:10.260
We talk about it being emotional, maybe spiritual.

03:10.720 --> 03:11.120
We do.

03:11.120 --> 03:21.000
But Arne Cox, in his work Music and Embodied Cognition, he suggests something much more concrete, actually, something that brings it right down into our physical existence.

03:21.280 --> 03:23.200
Okay, so what's his core idea then?

03:23.580 --> 03:28.120
Well, Cox's central argument is, it's pretty transformative, really.

03:28.520 --> 03:33.440
He says our experience and our deep understanding of music, it's not just about passive hearing.

03:33.440 --> 03:35.480
It's not just processing sound waves.

03:35.680 --> 03:35.740
Right.

03:36.140 --> 03:38.600
Instead, it's fundamentally rooted in the body.

03:39.600 --> 03:42.560
He argues that listening isn't just an auditory event.

03:42.760 --> 03:44.320
It's an active process.

03:44.660 --> 03:47.360
It involves what he calls embodied imitation.

03:47.900 --> 03:48.820
Embodied imitation.

03:49.020 --> 03:49.980
What does it mean exactly?

03:50.100 --> 03:51.520
Like mimicking the musician?

03:51.880 --> 03:52.460
Sort of, yeah.

03:52.800 --> 03:53.380
Think about it.

03:53.380 --> 04:01.300
When you listen to a really powerful opera singer hitting an incredible note, do you ever feel a tiny, subtle tension in your own throat?

04:01.700 --> 04:04.140
Like your vocal cords are almost trying to copy that effort.

04:04.340 --> 04:04.880
Oh, yeah.

04:05.140 --> 04:05.900
I definitely do that.

04:05.980 --> 04:10.300
Or like when I hear a really complex, fast drum solo, I feel it in my chest.

04:10.460 --> 04:10.540
Yeah.

04:10.540 --> 04:12.040
Or my hands start twitching.

04:12.140 --> 04:15.460
Like my body is trying to figure out the rhythm, even if I'm sitting totally still.

04:15.460 --> 04:17.520
Exactly that feeling.

04:17.940 --> 04:19.520
That's what Cox calls internal mimicry.

04:19.880 --> 04:27.620
We unconsciously, maybe even just subtly, we mimic the physical actions, the exertions that would be needed to make those sounds we're hearing.

04:27.800 --> 04:28.140
Huh.

04:28.780 --> 04:37.440
So hearing a virtuosic violin solo might, you know, unconsciously make you imagine the bowing motion, the dexterity, the pressure needed.

04:37.500 --> 04:37.760
Okay.

04:37.760 --> 04:44.840
Or a really powerful piano chord might make your fingers subtly tense up just a little as if you were hitting the keys yourself.

04:44.840 --> 04:45.360
Wow.

04:46.100 --> 04:48.700
And this isn't just like a poetic idea.

04:49.120 --> 04:53.020
Cox uses insights from neuroscience, from phenomenology, cognitive science.

04:53.700 --> 04:59.280
He argues that musical meaning is deeply fundamentally tied to this physical engagement, this internal mirroring.

04:59.320 --> 05:00.340
So it's not just listening.

05:00.440 --> 05:02.040
It's almost like a shared bodily dance.

05:02.120 --> 05:02.220
Yeah.

05:02.220 --> 05:06.700
An internal mirroring of what the musician is doing, even if we're not actually moving.

05:06.940 --> 05:07.460
Precisely.

05:07.820 --> 05:09.080
And Cox takes it even further.

05:09.200 --> 05:12.180
He suggests that listeners actively co-perform the music.

05:12.360 --> 05:13.020
Co-perform.

05:13.020 --> 05:18.240
Even if you're physically still, your body and mind are engaged in this imagined, this vicarious response.

05:18.480 --> 05:22.180
That's why musical meaning can feel so incredibly deep and, you know, visceral.

05:22.540 --> 05:23.440
Ah, okay.

05:23.520 --> 05:28.840
It's directly connected to our physical being, our potential for movement, our ability to act.

05:28.840 --> 05:34.840
It really transforms listening from just receiving sound into an active internal participation.

05:34.840 --> 05:35.840
Okay.

05:35.840 --> 05:42.740
Building on that idea of internal mimicry, Cox then expands it into something called mimetic proxy theory.

05:42.980 --> 05:44.660
This sounds like it goes beyond just music.

05:44.880 --> 05:45.400
It does, yeah.

05:45.540 --> 05:52.200
Mimetic proxy theory builds right on that mimetic hypothesis, the idea we internally simulate the actions of music creation.

05:52.200 --> 05:55.040
But the proxy concept is really key here.

05:55.240 --> 05:56.460
Proxy, like a stand-in.

05:56.620 --> 05:56.940
Exactly.

05:57.180 --> 06:07.220
Our understanding of other people's actions, whether it's a musician playing or, you know, just watching someone reach for a cup, it relies on using our own bodies as proxies for theirs.

06:07.780 --> 06:11.080
It's like our brain runs this super fast unconscious simulation.

06:11.580 --> 06:13.360
Okay, if I were doing that action, what would it feel like?

06:13.420 --> 06:14.300
What muscles would I use?

06:14.360 --> 06:15.520
How much effort would it take?

06:15.520 --> 06:23.100
So when I'm listening to an amazing guitarist, my brain isn't just hearing the notes, it's also activating the neural pathways I would use if I were playing those notes.

06:23.540 --> 06:27.320
My body becomes a kind of internal simulator for the guitarist's actions?

06:27.700 --> 06:28.600
That's exactly it.

06:28.640 --> 06:35.200
And this embodied simulation, it's absolutely crucial, not just for understanding music, but for empathy, for general social cognition.

06:35.400 --> 06:35.700
How?

06:36.020 --> 06:37.100
So for empathy?

06:37.100 --> 06:45.320
Well, it allows listeners to empathize really deeply with performers, to literally kind of feel their expressive intent behind the music.

06:45.520 --> 06:48.820
You don't just hear the sadness in a minor key melody.

06:49.020 --> 06:55.120
You subtly feel the imagined effort, the tension that would produce that specific emotional contour.

06:55.320 --> 06:55.740
Oh.

06:55.980 --> 06:59.500
It bridges the gap between the performer's inner state and your own.

06:59.640 --> 07:00.620
It's not just mimicry.

07:00.620 --> 07:09.320
It's like our bodies become these literal empathy machines, allowing us to feel and understand another person's experience, sometimes without a single word being spoken.

07:09.320 --> 07:15.740
This idea of internal co-performance, embodied empathy, it really does sound like what we were calling mind-melding.

07:15.920 --> 07:21.400
And you're saying it gets even stronger when we bring in an analogy from biology, horizontal gene transfer.

07:21.700 --> 07:23.420
That's a really profound connection, yeah.

07:23.500 --> 07:25.160
And it's quite intentional in Cox's thinking.

07:25.460 --> 07:30.200
In biology, horizontal gene transfer, or HGT, it's a pretty amazing process.

07:30.320 --> 07:31.280
How does it work generally?

07:31.660 --> 07:33.020
Well, normally you have vertical transmission.

07:33.020 --> 07:34.840
Parents pass genes to offspring.

07:35.100 --> 07:35.940
HGT is different.

07:36.480 --> 07:43.880
It allows for the non-linear, often really rapid movement of genetic material between organisms, even between different species sometimes.

07:44.140 --> 07:44.440
Wow.

07:44.720 --> 07:49.080
It's how bacteria, for instance, can quickly share traits like antibiotic resistance through a population.

07:49.680 --> 07:52.260
It bypasses generations of traditional inheritance.

07:52.960 --> 07:55.400
It's like a biological shortcut to getting new abilities.

07:55.400 --> 07:57.180
Okay, so how does that apply to music?

07:57.360 --> 08:04.260
You're suggesting music functions like a kind of cultural HGT, musical ideas, feelings transferred sideways, not just taught top-down.

08:04.520 --> 08:05.260
Precisely that.

08:06.020 --> 08:10.720
In Cox's view, music acts as a kind of cultural or maybe cognitive horizontal transfer.

08:11.460 --> 08:21.580
Musical ideas, specific gestures, even complex emotional states get transmitted between people, not through, you know, formal lessons or direct instruction over years.

08:21.660 --> 08:22.360
But how then?

08:22.360 --> 08:32.800
Through this embodied imitation, this shared visceral experience, it's a rapid, almost unconscious transmission of really complex information and feeling.

08:33.180 --> 08:39.720
Think about, like, everyone swaying together at a concert or how a really strong rhythm can feel like it synchronizes everyone in the room.

08:40.080 --> 08:42.080
That's like cultural HGT in action.

08:42.080 --> 08:47.120
And this truly feels like mind-melding because listeners aren't just passive receivers.

08:47.440 --> 08:53.560
They're internalizing, reproducing the performer's expressive state, creating this shared understanding.

08:53.800 --> 08:55.520
Yes, that's an excellent way to capture it.

08:55.740 --> 09:00.860
This process genuinely resembles mind-melding in a, well, in a pretty profound way.

09:01.320 --> 09:02.560
The listener isn't just observing.

09:02.940 --> 09:07.500
They become an active participant, their body and mind locked in this vicarious co-performance.

09:07.740 --> 09:09.400
So it creates a kind of collective consciousness.

09:09.400 --> 09:10.660
You could call it that.

09:11.020 --> 09:18.460
It fosters a shared, distributed understanding, a collective cognition, maybe, that definitely transcends individual minds.

09:19.280 --> 09:27.240
Just like HGT blurs the lines between biological species, this mimetic engagement in music blurs the boundaries between the performer and the listener.

09:27.620 --> 09:30.120
It fosters this powerful collective empathy.

09:30.580 --> 09:31.960
You're not just you listening to them.

09:31.960 --> 09:37.220
You become part of this larger shared musical happening, experiencing the music with them.

09:37.580 --> 09:42.220
It's a powerful reminder of how deeply our bodies are wired for connection and understanding.

09:42.720 --> 09:54.980
Okay, so if music can bring minds or many bodies together in this incredible, almost biological way, what happens when that potential for mind-melding just completely breaks down, when the rhythms just don't sync up?

09:55.080 --> 09:56.900
Right, when the connection fractures instead of forms.

09:56.900 --> 10:05.980
Let's pivot now to the world of philosophy, specifically to two thinkers who are almost always mentioned together, seen as these inseparable allies of post-structuralist thought.

10:06.420 --> 10:08.280
Michel Foucault and Gilles Deleuze.

10:08.560 --> 10:10.120
Yeah, that's the standard narrative, isn't it?

10:10.560 --> 10:18.440
You read introductions to theory, and they're often presented as this dynamic duo, sharing similar trajectories, fighting the same intellectual battles.

10:18.860 --> 10:20.540
They even work together on things.

10:20.540 --> 10:26.740
There's a twist. We're looking at research by Wendy Grace, who argues this common view is actually pretty misleading.

10:27.200 --> 10:30.580
She says they were, in fact, false friends, philosophically.

10:30.760 --> 10:40.200
Mm-hmm, that underneath the surface, there were significant, maybe unspoken, disagreements that ran really deep and ultimately proved, well, irreconcilable.

10:40.400 --> 10:43.720
And Grace pinpoints a specific moment, a breaking point.

10:43.880 --> 10:44.460
She does.

10:44.460 --> 10:49.920
Her research traces this simmering tension back to a specific year, 1976.

10:50.640 --> 10:55.120
That's when Deleuze wrote some critical notes on Foucault's La Volontée de Savoir.

10:55.220 --> 10:58.200
That's the first volume of the history of sexuality, right?

10:58.540 --> 10:58.940
Exactly.

10:59.540 --> 11:05.520
Those notes, Deleuze wrote, were eventually published much later in 94 as an essay called Desire and Pleasure.

11:06.200 --> 11:12.740
But according to Grace, Foucault read them back in 76, and the philosophical gap they revealed was just too wide.

11:12.740 --> 11:15.780
It apparently caused a deep, permanent rift.

11:16.640 --> 11:20.460
They essentially stopped speaking after that, a complete breakdown in their intellectual connection.

11:21.280 --> 11:21.640
Wow.

11:21.920 --> 11:29.700
So here are these towering figures who had influenced each other, collaborated, but they had this profound falling out over their core ideas.

11:30.180 --> 11:33.700
Not just a minor academic spat, but a real conceptual divorce.

11:34.240 --> 11:34.920
It seems so.

11:35.260 --> 11:42.000
And Grace even found an unpublished interview where Foucault, very unusually for him, explicitly stated his disagreement with Deleuze.

11:42.000 --> 11:42.960
Why is that unusual?

11:43.780 --> 11:47.040
Well, Foucault generally avoided criticizing specific individuals by name.

11:47.440 --> 11:49.660
He preferred to engage with ideas, systems of thought.

11:49.820 --> 11:54.100
So for him to single out Deleuze like that really underscores how deep this disagreement must have been.

11:54.420 --> 11:57.700
It highlights just how incompatible their fundamental ways of thinking had become.

11:58.120 --> 11:59.760
Hashtag, hashtag, tag 2.2.

11:59.960 --> 12:01.420
Different Nietzsche's different philosophies.

12:01.720 --> 12:08.420
Okay, to really get a grip on why they split so dramatically, we need to look at someone who influenced both of them immensely.

12:09.420 --> 12:10.160
Friedrich Nietzsche.

12:10.160 --> 12:13.220
Friedrich Nietzsche was pivotal for both.

12:13.880 --> 12:28.480
After World War II, there was this really important effort in French intellectual circles to denazify Nietzsche, to rescue his philosophy from how it had been misused and misrepresented by the Nazis.

12:28.720 --> 12:29.020
Right.

12:29.160 --> 12:34.340
Figures like Georges Bataille were crucial there, insisting fascism and Nietzsche were completely opposed.

12:34.340 --> 12:37.460
And Foucault and Deleuze were both deeply involved in this.

12:37.560 --> 12:42.640
They didn't just draw on Nietzsche to steer French thought away from the dominant Hegelianism of the time.

12:42.860 --> 12:49.460
They actually collaborated on the big authoritative French edition of Nietzsche's complete works, the Colley-Montaneri edition.

12:49.700 --> 12:51.240
They even co-wrote part of the preface.

12:51.400 --> 12:53.680
So they were working closely with the same source material?

12:53.680 --> 13:03.300
They were, but, and this is crucial, which Nietzsche they focused on, and more importantly, how they interpreted and used his ideas, turned out to be radically different.

13:03.640 --> 13:06.400
This is where their conceptual rhythms really started to diverge.

13:06.460 --> 13:07.900
Okay, how did Deleuze read Nietzsche?

13:08.060 --> 13:08.760
What was his take?

13:08.760 --> 13:16.680
For Deleuze, Nietzsche was primarily what he called a counter-philosopher, or sometimes a philosoph-maudit, a kind of cursed philosopher.

13:16.820 --> 13:17.060
Meaning?

13:17.360 --> 13:26.480
Meaning someone who challenges the very foundations of Western philosophy, but does so while remaining within that philosophical tradition, acting as its internal critic.

13:27.220 --> 13:30.120
For Deleuze, the core concept was difference.

13:30.620 --> 13:35.060
He saw it as internal, immediate, non-representational.

13:35.660 --> 13:38.320
Something to be explored using philosophical tools.

13:39.000 --> 13:42.900
Nietzsche gave him the means for an anti-representational critique from inside philosophy.

13:43.380 --> 13:47.440
It was about pure difference, before concepts or categories imposed themselves.

13:47.800 --> 13:51.180
Okay, so Deleuze stays within philosophy, using Nietzsche to challenge it.

13:51.420 --> 13:53.140
How did Foucault see Nietzsche differently?

13:53.580 --> 13:55.300
Foucault's Nietzsche was quite different.

13:55.540 --> 13:57.920
He admired Nietzsche more as an ethnographer of the present.

13:57.920 --> 14:01.260
For Foucault, Nietzsche wasn't primarily a traditional philosopher.

14:01.420 --> 14:08.100
They're more like a groundbreaking genealogist who started a whole new way of doing history, a history of Western culture and thought.

14:08.100 --> 14:09.900
So moving beyond philosophy.

14:10.480 --> 14:10.880
Exactly.

14:11.500 --> 14:17.120
Moving into what Foucault later called his archaeology of thought, or history of systems of thought.

14:17.380 --> 14:21.160
For Foucault, difference wasn't some pure internal thing.

14:21.380 --> 14:26.920
It was always external, always dependent on specific historical representational truth regimes.

14:27.480 --> 14:31.460
The ways power and knowledge define what counts as true or normal at a given time.

14:31.460 --> 14:37.160
And you uncover these differences through detailed historical, almost ethnographic, comparative work.

14:37.620 --> 14:40.260
He wasn't chasing a pure philosophical difference.

14:40.580 --> 14:45.180
He wanted to know how differences were actively produced and managed by power throughout history.

14:45.560 --> 14:49.240
So completely different projects, really, even though both were inspired by Nietzsche.

14:49.440 --> 14:52.960
Fundamentally different readings, leading to fundamentally different intellectual trajectories.

14:53.160 --> 14:58.100
But despite these deep differences in their core readings, they did share some important ground, didn't they?

14:58.100 --> 15:00.120
You mentioned collaboration, activism.

15:00.220 --> 15:00.780
Oh, absolutely.

15:01.240 --> 15:07.880
Especially after the major upheaval of May 1968 in France, they both developed this idea of the specific intellectual.

15:08.320 --> 15:10.940
As opposed to the universal intellectual, like Sartre.

15:11.180 --> 15:11.740
Precisely.

15:12.340 --> 15:17.100
They moved away from the model of the intellectual who claims authority to speak on everything.

15:17.100 --> 15:28.720
Their shared insight, which Deleuze famously termed the indignity of speaking for others, was that intellectuals should speak from their specific knowledge and experience, not claim to speak for entire groups.

15:28.920 --> 15:29.260
Okay.

15:29.260 --> 15:39.240
And this guided their activism, most famously with the GIP, the Group d'Information sur les Prisons, Prisons Information Group, which Foucault's partner, Daniel Deferre, helped start.

15:39.660 --> 15:41.400
They weren't trying to be the voice of prisoners.

15:41.540 --> 15:43.760
They worked to enable prisoners to speak for themselves.

15:44.000 --> 15:44.140
Right.

15:44.140 --> 15:47.740
They also shared a strong anti-psychoanalytic stance.

15:48.560 --> 15:55.360
Both saw psychoanalysis less as liberation and more as a subtle form of social control, a way of normalizing people.

15:55.920 --> 16:03.080
So, yes, there was significant common ground, shared enemies, shared projects, which makes their eventual split even more striking, really.

16:03.080 --> 16:12.520
So, okay, despite all that shared history, collaboration, activism, Wendy Grace argues the big 1976 split wasn't just about abstract philosophical differences.

16:12.920 --> 16:18.260
It was over something very specific, very concrete, and maybe deeply personal, the nature of sexuality itself.

16:18.260 --> 16:22.720
That is absolutely Grace's central and, I think, most powerful argument.

16:23.820 --> 16:34.360
She contends that the intellectual break was fundamentally about sexuality, not just as a topic to study, but as a theoretical framework, and crucially, as a lived experience.

16:34.560 --> 16:36.560
Lived experience being key here.

16:36.560 --> 16:36.900
Yes.

16:36.900 --> 16:45.080
She makes a very compelling case that Foucault's homosexuality gave him a radically different perspective on sexuality compared to the heterosexual, Deleuze.

16:46.120 --> 16:53.760
And that this personal lens, this lived reality, deeply shaped their philosophical approaches to what sexuality even is.

16:53.760 --> 16:58.720
This difference, she argues, ultimately created an insurmountable conceptual barrier between them.

16:58.980 --> 17:07.480
It's really fascinating how personal identity and experience could fuel such a major intellectual divergence, creating that kind of conceptual friction or torsion we mentioned.

17:08.000 --> 17:12.080
And Grace's sexuality wasn't just a late interest for Foucault with the history of sexuality books.

17:12.140 --> 17:12.620
Not at all.

17:12.720 --> 17:14.980
She shows it was central right from his early work.

17:15.460 --> 17:22.700
His huge 1961 book, History of Madness, already used sexuality as a primary example to illustrate his unique historical method.

17:22.700 --> 17:40.640
Well, in that book, he explored how the classical age in Europe actively created new forms of intolerance towards male homosexuality, and how things like erudite libertinage, these intense, lyrical, same-sex friendships common before effectively vanished after the Renaissance.

17:41.440 --> 17:45.680
For Foucault, the very categories we used for sexuality weren't natural facts.

17:45.680 --> 17:52.360
They were historically constructed, enforced through power, shaping how people could even think about or experience their desires.

17:52.880 --> 17:58.080
Okay, so Foucault is deeply committed to seeing sexuality as historically constructed, shaped by power.

17:58.940 --> 18:04.120
Where did Deleuze, maybe unintentionally, step on a landmine for him in those 1976 notes?

18:04.400 --> 18:06.080
What was the passage that caused the break?

18:06.460 --> 18:10.400
Grace identifies a really crucial sentence in Deleuze's notes, the desire and pleasure piece.

18:10.940 --> 18:17.040
He's discussing desire and mentions a body without organs defined solely by zones of intensity limits, gradients, fluxes.

18:17.360 --> 18:19.660
Now, body without organs is a famous Deleuzean idea.

18:19.660 --> 18:22.340
Right, representing a fluid pre-individual potential.

18:22.440 --> 18:22.820
Exactly.

18:23.060 --> 18:26.920
But then Deleuze adds, this body is just as much biological as collective and political.

18:27.160 --> 18:29.920
And it's that word biological that Grace argues was the trigger.

18:30.280 --> 18:33.520
Why was biological such a red flag for Foucault?

18:33.660 --> 18:36.740
Why did it clash so fundamentally with his whole project?

18:36.980 --> 18:47.780
Because Foucault had spent his entire intellectual life systematically challenging precisely those kinds of biological or natural explanations for subjectivity for human experience.

18:47.780 --> 18:54.420
He was fiercely critical of the way science was used to define and categorize human beings, especially regarding sexuality.

18:54.920 --> 18:59.060
He argued vehemently against any natural or essential basis for sexuality.

18:59.420 --> 19:00.780
